---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../site.mjs';
import { TOOLS } from '../../learn.mjs';
const tool = TOOLS[1];
const url = `${SITE}/tools/${tool.slug}`;
const jsonld = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'WebPage',
      '@id': `${url}#webpage`,
      url,
      name: tool.title,
      inLanguage: 'en',
      isPartOf: { '@id': `${SITE}/#website` },
      mainEntity: { '@id': `${url}#app` },
      breadcrumb: {
        '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
        itemListElement: [
          { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE}/` },
          { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Tools', item: `${SITE}/tools` },
          { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: tool.name, item: url },
        ],
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'WebApplication',
      '@id': `${url}#app`,
      name: tool.name,
      url,
      applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Any',
      browserRequirements: 'Requires JavaScript',
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      description: tool.short,
      publisher: { '@id': `${SITE}/#organization` },
    },
  ],
};
---
<Base title={tool.title} description={tool.description} canonical={url} jsonld={jsonld}
  css={['/site/v5/orvum-public.css', '/site/v5/orvum-public-2.css', '/site/css/pages.css', '/site/css/learn.css']}>
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
<Nav current="/tools" />
<main id="main">
<section class="sec dark page-top" aria-labelledby="h1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Crumbs items={[{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, { href: '/tools', label: 'Tools' }, { label: tool.name }]} />
    <h1 id="h1">Sell-through rate calculator</h1>
    <p class="lead">Sell-through rate shows how much of the stock you received has actually sold. Enter units (or value) to see sell-through, your weekly selling pace and how many weeks of stock you have left.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec light" aria-label="Calculator">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="calc" id="st">
      <form novalidate>
        <div class="calc-opts">
          <fieldset><legend>Measure in</legend>
            <label><input type="radio" name="unit" value="units" checked> Units</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="unit" value="value"> Value</label>
          </fieldset>
          <fieldset data-basis hidden><legend>Value basis</legend>
            <label><input type="radio" name="basis" value="cost" checked> At cost</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="basis" value="retail"> At original retail (before markdowns)</label>
          </fieldset>
        </div>
        <p class="hint">Use the same period and scope for every input — for example, season to date for one brand. Count sales net of customer returns; transfers between stores are not sales.</p>
        <div class="grid">
          <label for="st-rec">Received (<span data-u>units</span>) <span class="hint">Required.</span></label><input id="st-rec" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off">
          <label for="st-sold">Sold, net of returns (<span data-u>units</span>) <span class="hint">Required.</span></label><input id="st-sold" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off">
          <label for="st-beg">Beginning inventory (<span data-u>units</span>) <span class="hint">Optional: stock carried in from before the period.</span></label><input id="st-beg" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off">
          <label for="st-wk">Weeks elapsed <span class="hint">Optional: for weekly pace and weeks of supply.</span></label><input id="st-wk" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off">
        </div>
        <p class="calc-go"><button type="submit" class="pbtn primary">Calculate</button></p>
      </form>
      <div class="calc-out" aria-live="polite"></div>
      <noscript><p class="calc-msg">The calculator needs JavaScript. The formulas and a worked example are below.</p></noscript>
      <p class="note">Runs in your browser: nothing you enter is sent or saved.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec light" aria-labelledby="how-h" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h2 id="how-h">How to calculate sell-through rate</h2>
    <p class="formula">Sell-through rate = units sold ÷ units received × 100.</p>
    <p>If you also carry stock from before the period, a stricter version divides by everything available: units sold ÷ (beginning inventory + units received) × 100. The calculator shows both, so you can compare like with like.</p>
    <p>Use the same period and scope for every input (for example, season to date for one brand). Count units sold net of customer returns, and do not count transfers between stores as sales. In value mode, measure received and sold stock on the same basis: cost, or original retail price before markdowns.</p>
    <h2>Example <span class="tag">illustrative</span></h2>
    <p>A boutique received 200 units of a brand's season and sold 130 in the first 6 weeks.</p>
    <p class="formula">Sell-through = 130 ÷ 200 = 65%.<br>With 50 units carried over from last season, sell-through on everything available = 130 ÷ 250 = 52%.</p>
    <p>It sells about 21.7 units a week, and the 120 units on hand cover about 5.5 weeks.</p>
    <h2>Reading sell-through in multi-brand retail</h2>
    <p>Compare brands and categories at the same point in the season, using the same version of the formula. A brand with high sell-through early may deserve a re-order; one with low sell-through close to the markdown period needs a decision on price or transfer. Very high sell-through can also mean the buy was too shallow and sales were lost.</p>
    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <h3>What is a good sell-through rate?</h3>
    <p>It depends on category, price point and how far into the season you are. The most useful benchmark is your own history for the same brand and period.</p>
    <h3>Should I use units or value?</h3>
    <p>Units show how products move; value shows how much money has turned back into sales. Use units for buying decisions and value for finance.</p>
    <h3>How is sell-through different from inventory turnover?</h3>
    <p>Sell-through measures one delivery or season. Inventory turnover measures how many times average stock sells over a year.</p>
    <p class="rel-line">Related: <a href="/glossary/sell-through-rate">Sell-through rate explained</a> · <a href="/glossary/weeks-of-supply">Weeks of supply</a> · <a href="/tools/open-to-buy-calculator">Open-to-buy calculator</a></p>
    <p class="note">ORVUM is an AI-native retail operating system in development, designed to track sell-through by brand and store from live sales and stock. <a href="/platform">See the platform →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>
</main>
<Footer />
<script is:inline src="/js/calc-st.js" defer></script>
</Base>
